    {"name": "hands_on_criticality", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.65},
    {"name": "resource_equity_gap", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.6,
  "rationale": "The query ('How can educators improve learning satisfaction?') is a genuine what-if/intervention intent, and the augmentation supplies multiple intervenable treatments that an educator can actually set: instruction delivery quality, peer/community presence, learner control over pace, and pedagogical innovation level. These pass the intervenability test (a). For (b), several columns capture common causes not named in the query -- subject_format_fit and hands_on_criticality are intrinsic subject properties that shape both the feasible interventions and satisfaction, and resource_equity_gap captures population/context disparities that confound the treatment-outcome relation. None of these confounders were named in the query (which only mentions educators and satisfaction), so they demonstrate the capability under test. The confounders are plausible but somewhat coarse and could arguably be treated as effect modifiers; hence moderate confounder quality rather than high."
}
